WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:06.530
SOPHIA HU: Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:10.360
I'm Sophia Hu, a data management
specialist based in Tokyo.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.110
Today I'm going to talk
about how Cloud Spanner helps

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:19.060
gaming companies organize their
gaming application and better

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.140
players' experience.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.780
ML can help you predict many
things today and it's amazing.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:31.520
But one thing it cannot predict
is a successful video game.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.640
So what are we
trying to solve here

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.500
with our database solutions?

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.510
Unpredictability.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.000
Compute is very easy to scale.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.030
You need more resource,
you add more server.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.520
When a game gets
a lot of players,

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.070
database is usually
the bottleneck.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.010
Because [INAUDIBLE]
DB like MySQL,

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:56.190
are not designed to
scale horizontally.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.470
How did gaming companies solve
the database scaling problem?

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:04.069
Well, by using a
technical called sharding.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:07.850
The first one shards
data by player's name.

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.730
The more you [INAUDIBLE]
the DB, the more

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.490
players your game will handle.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.590
Second one is realm sharding.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.980
This is typical in
MMORPG, where you

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.650
create new, parallel
worlds when you

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.960
need to handle more players.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.520
If a single database server
can handle 10,000 players,

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.910
then they have to manage
100 servers for each million

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.030
players.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.960
Last one is platform
sharding, where

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.945
you will separate players'
population by platform.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.090
What if you could simply
scale your game backend?

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:48.160
That's where Cloud
Spanner comes in.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.310
Cloud Spanner is the
first enterprise-grade,

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.910
globally-distributed, and
strongly-consistent database

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:59.080
service built for the
cloud specifically

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.250
to combine the benefit of
relational database structure

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.890
with non-relational
horizontal scale.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.490
What are the key benefits of
using Cloud Spanner for gaming

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.050
application?

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.750
Manually sharding makes
it difficult to maintain

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:18.550
consistency and
transactions across shards.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.980
Cloud Spanner gives
you relational data

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.080
and horizontal scaling.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.850
It optimizes performance by
automatically sharding the data

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.730
based on the load
and [? data ?] sites.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.390
Customers who are using
RDBMS want more power

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:38.900
and are considering a
scalable [INAUDIBLE] database.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.200
Spanner allows them to
keep their relational data

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.000
and can be scaled.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.490
Cloud Spanner has no planned
downtime for maintenance,

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:50.600
with fully automatic data
replication and server

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.310
redundancy.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:56.510
And it delivers up to
five 9s of availability

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.850
in multi-region instances.

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.840
Also, Cloud Spanner users
can make a schema change

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.080
while serving traffic
with zero downtime.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.960
With Spanner in a
multi-region configuration,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.140
any update is automatically
replicated across regions

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:18.020
so that you don't have to
add extra logic for that

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.880
in your application.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.600
Let's take a look
at some examples

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.115
of companies using Spanner.

00:03:25.115 --> 00:03:27.640
Niantic learned about
how Google Cloud

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.950
can help gaming
companies ensure they

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.490
have enough scale for a
surprise hit with "Pokemon Go."

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:39.640
"Pokemon Go" wildly exceeded
the team's capacity estimates

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:45.280
and they used Google Cloud
data store to scale to over 50

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.240
times their prediction
worst case scenario.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.360
After a few years,
Niantic migrated

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:55.240
from [INAUDIBLE]
to Spanner, which

00:03:55.240 --> 00:04:00.830
reduces their operational
cost and improved latency.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:05.840
Another example is "Dragon Ball
Legends" from Bandai Namco.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.510
With Cloud Spanner
as a backend DB,

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:13.460
BNE focused on developing
an amazing game instead

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:18.350
of spending time on database
capacity planning and scaling.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.769
By using a fully managed
scalable database,

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:24.680
they drastically
reduced the risk

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.430
related to human errors as
well as operational overhead.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.360
Let's take a quick deep
dive into a Spanner

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.630
and some of the benefits
of cloud native.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:39.140
The Google network offers
better reliability,

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.470
speed, and security
performance, as

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:45.800
compared with
non-deterministic performance

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.800
of the public internet
or other cloud networks.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.970
Spanner's capabilities
are augmented

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.820
by our global,
private fiber optic

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:00.320
network that stitches
together all of our services

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.740
and allows us to provide
low latency backend.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:10.270
Spanner has two type of
instances, regional or

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.280
multi-region instance.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:17.530
The diagram represents a
two-node regional instance.

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:20.260
For each node,
Cloud Spanner will

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:25.570
create three synchronous
replicas across zones.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:31.840
Each zone in the region contains
a few replica of the database.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.690
With multi-region
configuration, Cloud Spanner

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:39.020
will create up to nine
replicas for each node.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.530
These additional
replicas enable you

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:46.600
to read data with low latency
from multiple locations close

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.320
to or within the regions
in the configuration.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.980
Here I'd like to highlight
the new multi-region instance

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:58.810
configuration in Asia called
Asia1 with Tokyo, Osaka,

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.280
and Seoul region.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.500
It's often mentioned
that TrueTime

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.310
is essential to make Spanner
work as well as it does.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:09.260
So what is it, and
how does it help?

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:13.010
TrueTime is a way to
synchronize clocks

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:16.270
in all machines across
multiple data centers.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:21.640
The system uses a combination
of GPS and automatic clocks

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.800
to give an accurate
source of time

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.260
for all Google's application.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:29.800
Cloud Spanner assign a
timestamp to every read

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:31.720
and write transaction.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.060
Different nodes
can make decisions

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:38.740
about the order of transaction
operation made from other nodes

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:42.115
by simply comparing
these timestamps.

00:06:42.115 --> 00:06:45.850
It enables applications
to generate monotonically

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:51.040
in creating timestamps, which
allow the system to behave

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:55.410
as if all transactions
were executed sequentially,

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.560
even though Cloud Spanner
actually runs them

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.990
across multiple servers
in multiple data

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:05.190
centers for higher
performance and availability.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:10.010
It's a combination of Google's
fast, redundant global network

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:14.200
and TrueTime makes Google Cloud
Spanner external [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.030
possible.

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:19.790
So how do we spread the
data to Spanner nodes?

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:22.070
Each table in the
database is broken down

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:25.580
into several what we
call splits, used range

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.260
of the primary keys.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:30.960
All the data in a split is
physically stored together

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.510
in a replica.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.990
And Cloud Spanner servers
serve each replica an out

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.990
of independent failure zone.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.330
For example, this table is
separated into a three splits

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.350
using range of the keys.

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:50.250
In reality, the number of
splits can vary according

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:51.970
to the amount of the data.

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:55.860
And the splits are rebalanced
dynamically depending

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.410
on the amount of data and load.

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:03.720
So for the three node
instances we saw previously,

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.720
it could be managed
like this, going back

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:10.710
to our original scenario,
with each node handling

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.660
three of the splits.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.420
But remember that the
table and the nodes

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.820
are replicated
across three zones.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.570
How does that work?

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:21.630
Look across the three zones.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:26.540
Each split is replicated
in all three zones.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:30.690
Use Paxos consensus
protocols, one of the zones

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:33.220
is determined to be a leader.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:35.730
The leader is
responsible for managing

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:38.820
write transactions
for that split,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:42.309
while the other replicas
can be used for reads.

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:46.860
Every write operation must
pass a majority quorum vote.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:49.890
For different splits,
different zones

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:54.990
can become leaders, thus
distributing the leadership

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:59.320
roles among all of the
Cloud Spanner compute nodes.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:04.190
It's using the distributed
mechanism of splits, leaders,

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:07.360
and replicas that
Cloud Spanner gains

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.580
both its high availability
and scalability.

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.310
Let's talk about what
scalability looks

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.350
like in practice.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:20.730
By increasing the number
of nodes, you add capacity.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:26.020
A node can handle around 2,000
writes per second and 10,000

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:27.510
reads per second.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:31.650
Imagine we're approaching
launch date for our game.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:35.710
And based upon the throughput
capability of a Spanner node,

00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:39.630
they've determined that we
need a three-node cluster

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:44.010
for a total potential
threshold of 6,000

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:49.260
writes per second and
30,000 reads per second.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:52.080
Based upon our best
estimates, they've

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.690
determined that three
nodes of Spanner

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:59.490
should be sufficient for
anticipated launch date load.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:03.570
You can configure cost
performance capacity

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:05.220
with the number of nodes.

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:08.610
Shortly after
launch, the game goes

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:14.220
viral and traffic surges,
pushing the Spanner instance

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.950
passed performance thresholds.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:20.880
We can easily go in and
add an additional node

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.740
to our instance.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.400
Once a node has been
added to the instance,

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:31.100
the nodes add their
pointers to the splits,

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:32.940
[? node ?] is moved.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:37.470
Now with the additional node, we
have a new performance ceiling

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:43.460
of 40,000 reads per second
and 8,000 write per second,

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:46.410
and performance
begins to stabilize.

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:51.320
Crisis averted, and with
a minimum amount of fuss.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:55.200
No frantic re-architecting,
re-sharding,

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.720
re-platforming of the data.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:01.290
Behind the scene, this
is a Spanner dynamically

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:02.760
re-sharding the data.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:07.680
After some time, performance
returns to pre-surge levels

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:11.520
and we find ourself with
significant additional

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:12.780
capacity.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.680
To help save cost, we decided
to add it to the instance

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:21.250
and remove the additional
node that had been added.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:24.940
So far, I have described
some key concept

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:28.720
and advantages of Spanner
for gaming customers.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:32.800
I hope I could give you a quick
overview regarding how Spanner

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:37.390
can help gaming applications
with strong consistency,

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:40.840
dynamic scale, global
data distribution,

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:42.070
and live operation.

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:45.340
Cloud Spanner has been
tremendously adopted

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:49.720
in gaming industry and
I'm so excited to have

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.990
one of our very cool
customers, Colopl,

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:57.420
here today to share their
story with Cloud Spanner.

