00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:06.700
Here's the agenda
for what I'm going

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:08.610
to be talking about today.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:13.490
First, I will start by talking
about why we chose Spanner.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:15.760
Then I will talk about
how we integrated Spanner

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:17.690
into our development.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:19.880
After that, I will
provide some tips

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:22.970
on how to use Spanner
in real life scenarios.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:26.550
So now I'll talk about why
we Spanner for this game.

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:28.080
There are three reasons.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:31.920
Scalability, cost,
and stability.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:35.460
Starting with the first one
on the list, scalability.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.870
We used to use MySQL to
handle all user data.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:42.580
And we scaled it by splitting
data into horizontal shards.

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:46.330
This worked well for a while,
but when the numbers of shards

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:48.070
grew to double
digits, we started

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:49.900
to notice some problems
with this approach

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:52.570
whenever we wanted to scale.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:55.100
Scaling in and out
took days to prepare,

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:56.530
and we scaled every month.

00:13:56.530 --> 00:13:59.440
So we would scale out whenever
we released a new feature

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.550
to handle the high traffic.

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:03.820
And then we would
scale in to save costs

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:05.712
once the traffic eased.

00:14:05.712 --> 00:14:09.410
By switching to Spanner, we were
able to do this with one click

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:13.030
and scaling would complete
within a few minutes.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:15.730
The second reason is cost.

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:17.870
Like I mentioned in
the previous slide,

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:20.600
we used to spend
days scaling MySQL.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:23.650
But switching to Spanner
enabled us to reduce the time

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:26.650
it takes from days
to minutes, and that

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:29.540
reduced the cost of
operation quite a bit.

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:33.040
Another place we save costs
is from code maintenance.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:35.220
Sharding made the code
complex and difficult

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.560
to maintain because the
code had to accommodate

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:41.620
for things like distributed
transactions and replication

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.030
delays.

00:14:43.030 --> 00:14:46.275
These things made it costly
to add features and fix bugs.

00:14:46.275 --> 00:14:49.120
But using Spanner allowed
us to simplify the code

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:53.190
so adding features and fixing
bugs can be done a lot cheaper.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:57.590
The third reason we chose
Spanner was for stability.

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:01.570
As mentioned by Sophia, Spanner
has at least four 9 SLA,

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.170
which is phenomenal.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:05.440
When we self-managed
MySQL servers, which

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.320
amounted to hundreds,
a small number of them

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.100
used to crash randomly
at a constant rate.

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:15.950
And having these crashes
used to cause a lot of grief.

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.860
After switching to
Spanner, we haven't had

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.210
any of these types of crashes.

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.460
We've been running this
game for 10 months now,

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:25.870
and we haven't had
any major downtimes.

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.410
Now, I would like to talk
about how we integrated Spanner

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:30.570
into the game's development.

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:34.050
Here's the architecture overview
of how it looked like before we

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:35.850
started using Spanner.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:39.900
As you can see, MySQL was used
to store all persistent data

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.320
we used in the game.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:45.660
And here's the architecture
overview after the integration.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:47.730
As you can see,
Spanner replaced MySQL

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:50.210
for storing persistent data.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:52.140
You might have noticed
that MySQL's still

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:53.990
used for shared data.

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.500
I'll be explaining that
later in the presentation.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:59.240
So now I'll explain how we
actually did the integration,

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:01.470
starting with some background.

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.570
When we first decided
to integrate Spanner

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.100
into the game, we
knew it was going

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:07.680
to be a challenge, since
the game was already

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:10.132
in development using MySQL.

00:16:10.132 --> 00:16:11.590
So we did some
preparations to make

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:13.632
sure this transition would
be smooth and painless

00:16:13.632 --> 00:16:15.050
as possible.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:17.620
So here is the process we
took to integrate Spanner

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:19.320
into the model.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.880
First, we started with
a small dedicated team

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:25.790
that ran some basic benchmarks
to check the performance.

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:28.550
Once that was good, we
created a dedicated driver

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:32.470
for the PHP framework we
were using called Laravel.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:35.930
Doing so allowed us to use
the framework's ORM, which

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:37.700
also made it possible
to use extensions

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:40.680
we built in other games.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:43.030
So once the driver was
written, we tested the driver

00:16:43.030 --> 00:16:45.880
by replacing MySQL driver
with a Spanner driver

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:47.200
on an existing game.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:49.750
This method worked
well because you

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:53.240
got to see how it performed
in real life scenarios.

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:56.055
After that was done, we joined
the game development team

00:16:56.055 --> 00:16:59.500
to educate them through
learning workshops.

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:02.880
So by doing these preparations,
the game development team

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:06.355
was able to fully integrate
Spanner in about two weeks.

00:17:06.355 --> 00:17:08.230
Now, I'd like to give
you some tips for using

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:10.513
Spanner in real life scenarios.

00:17:10.513 --> 00:17:13.750
The first item on the list
is handling bursts of access

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:16.410
to shared data.

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:18.510
So our game had some
small sets of data which

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:20.940
is shared across all users.

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:23.010
These are things
like item properties

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.480
and monster map coordinates.

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:28.180
Now, if we access
these data in Spanner,

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:31.750
we would get what's called a
hotspot in the database, which

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:35.520
caused latency to shoot up.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:38.120
This happens because all the
I/O is happening on one slice

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:40.400
of data that cannot
be distributed,

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:43.150
as you can see in this slide.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:45.820
This is troubling, but there
is a way to work around this.

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:48.250
The way to handle this
kind of data in Spanner

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:50.740
is to add a column
with a shard number

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.650
and insert copies of the data
with shard numbers assigned

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:54.400
to them.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.170
Then what you can do is have
the app select one of the shards

00:17:57.170 --> 00:18:00.200
at random and add
that to the query.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:02.100
And that will
distribute the load.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:05.600
This implementation works,
but unfortunately, we

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:07.850
did not use this
solution in the game,

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:10.190
because we figured it was
too difficult to implement

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:11.870
into the framework at the time.

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:15.110
So our actual solution to
this problem looks like this.

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:18.680
Yes, we decided to use a
self-managed MySQL instead,

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:21.320
since MySQL can replicate
and scale out easily

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:25.192
if all you have is that
small set of read-only data.

00:18:25.192 --> 00:18:26.900
The next thing I would
like to talk about

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.120
is handling launch day traffic.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:33.140
So as described earlier, when
there is high load on a split,

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:35.902
it adds more boundaries
to distribute the load.

00:18:35.902 --> 00:18:37.610
This works well in
normal scenarios where

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:39.710
there is a gradual
growth on load,

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:41.150
but this was not
what we expected

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:43.010
to happen on launch day.

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:45.130
No, what we expected
to happen on launch day

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:47.220
was thousands of
users registering

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.720
in a span of a few seconds.

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:52.080
So we did a simulation
of launch day traffic

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:55.080
by ramping up game bots
that mimicked user behavior,

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:57.000
and this is how it turned out.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:00.300
Immediately after we started
the test, the CPU usage shot up

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:03.240
and latency of the queries
increased to the point

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:04.500
where it was unplayable.

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:07.020
The reason this happened was
because Spanner could not

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:10.050
make the splits fast enough to
handle the huge amount of data

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:11.220
that was being inserted.

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.620
So we concluded that in
order to make Spanner handle

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:15.780
this massive traffic,
we needed to make

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.670
the splits before the launch.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:20.330
So this is how we
resolved the issue.

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:22.890
We fed Spanner just enough
queries to allow the load base

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:25.860
split to trigger, and
increased the number of queries

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:28.660
gradually until there was enough
splits to handle the desired

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:29.580
PPS.

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.600
This was a tricky process,
because if we fed it too many,

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:34.830
the CPU would starve,
which slowed down

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:36.870
the splitting process.

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:38.610
But if we gave it
too little, there

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:41.710
wouldn't be enough load
to trigger the split.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:43.680
So we had to monitor
the metrics constantly

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:47.620
to ensure that we fed Spanner
the right amount of queries.

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:51.040
Since this was a tedious task
that took hours to complete,

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:55.696
we made a warm-up app that
automated the whole process.

00:19:55.696 --> 00:19:57.220
And here's an
illustration of what

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:59.530
the whole process looks like.

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:02.430
So in addition to what
I've just described,

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:03.900
there are two
things you should be

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:07.336
aware of when doing a warm-up.

00:20:07.336 --> 00:20:12.060
So number one is do not drop
the tables after the warm-up.

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:14.880
In most cases, you would want
to delete dummy records that

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:16.820
were inserted
during the warm-up,

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:18.930
but don't do it by
dropping the tables,

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:21.810
because that will
also clear the splits.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:24.270
You should delete
individual records instead,

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:26.922
since that will allow
the splits to remain.

00:20:26.922 --> 00:20:28.380
So the second thing
you should know

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:32.096
is to warm up 24 hours
before the launch.

00:20:32.096 --> 00:20:34.960
Spanner seems to merge the
splits if they aren't being

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:37.780
accessible over 48
hours, so make sure you

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:41.460
warm up your database 48
hours prior to launch.

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:44.860
So the last thing I want to
advise is how to scale in.

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:48.310
So in this game, we scaled
in and out fairly often

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:51.070
based on how much traffic
we're expecting for the day.

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:53.380
Scaling out a simple,
since all you have to do

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:56.050
is increase the number
of nodes in the UI

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:58.330
and wait a few minutes
for it to be deployed.

00:20:58.330 --> 00:21:01.210
Scaling in, however,
is a bit more tricky.

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:04.090
When we scale in by
reducing the number of nodes

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:07.390
too aggressively, we
noticed that the latency

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:09.660
increase for some users.

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:12.400
These latencies ranged
from a few milliseconds up

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:15.330
to something like 30 seconds.

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:17.750
So we resolved this
issue by forcing Spanner

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:21.510
to time-out in three seconds and
return a retry response, which

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:24.020
asked the client to retry
after a few seconds.

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:26.120
In addition to that, we
now reduce the number

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:29.090
of nodes in small
intervals, since the latency

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:31.940
seems to be proportional to
the number of nodes we reduced.

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:35.660
We managed to minimize the delay
enough that no retries occurred

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:39.110
on the client by reducing the
number to one node per five

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:40.080
minutes.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.080
So the takeaway
here is, don't try

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:44.640
to reduce the number
of nodes in one go,

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:47.390
but reduce them gradually
in small intervals.

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:49.400
That's all I have for you today.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:50.970
Thank you for your time.

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:53.620
Handing it back to you, Sophia.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:56.970
SOPHIA HU: Thank you,
Taka, for your great story.

00:21:56.970 --> 00:22:00.660
For the next steps, we
have a few more sessions

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:01.860
about Spanner.

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:06.220
So if you have time,
please watch them as well.

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:10.620
Also, we have Demo and
Qwiklab about Spanner as well.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:12.510
Please try them out.

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:15.420
OK, that's all
for today from us.

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:17.420
Thank you for watching.

